<worksheet>
    <title>Lab 5, Due: December ??, 2026</title>
    
    <introduction>
        <p>
            <term>Instructions:</term> Complete the following exercises in your
            groups.
            For the write up:
        </p>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Include descriptions of your work in Sage.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        You only need to hand in one report for your group.
                        Only include the names of students who contributed.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        This lab is graded out of 30 points.
                        The number of points for each part 
                        are indicated next to the problem. You earn full credit 
                        by including your reasoning, following all
                        directions, and making only minor errors.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
<p>
    In Lab 2, we modeled the distribution of
    bicycles between two locations in Grand Rapids, given probabilities that a
    bike rented from one location was returned to one location or the other.
    In so doing, we found that we could represent the distribution of bikes on
    day <m>k</m> by the vector <m>\xvec_{k}</m>, and that we could determine a
    matrix <m>A</m> so that the distribution of bikes on the next day was given
    by
</p>

<p>
    <md>
        \xvec_{k+1}= A \xvec_{k}
    </md>
</p>

<p>
    We call the matrix <m>A</m> a <em>transition matrix</em>, and the equation
    <m>\xvec_{k+1}= A \xvec_{k}</m> a <em>discrete dynamical system</em>.
    Note that the matrix <m>A</m> enables us to move from <q>state</q>
    <m>x_{k}</m> to <q>state</q> <m>x_{k+1}</m> by simply multiplying by
    <m>A</m>.
    In this lab activity, we explore discrete dynamical systems that model how
    two species of animals interact with one another, and study how the
    eigenvalues and eigenvectors of <m>A</m> enable us to understand and predict
    long-range behavior of populations.
</p>

<p>
    A famous example in mathematical biology involves the interactions of moose and wolves on Isle Royale
    <url href="https://isleroyalewolf.org/overview/overview/at_a_glance.html">
    </url>
    in Lake Superior.
    If we let <m>R_{k}</m> be the number of moose on the island in year <m>k</m>
    and <m>S_{k}</m> the number of wolves in year <m>k</m>, it makes sense that
    the numbers of each in the next year, <m>R_{k+1}</m> and <m>S_{k+1}</m>
    should depend on some combination of <m>R_{k}</m> and <m>S_{k}</m>.
    This leads us to think about the equations
    <md alignment="align">
        <mrow>R_{k+1} &amp; = &amp; aR_k + bS_k</mrow>
        <mrow>S_{k+1} &amp; = &amp; cR_k + dS_k</mrow>
    </md>
    for some constants <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, <m>c</m>, and <m>d</m> that depend on
    how the species <m>R</m> and <m>S</m> interact with each other.
    If we let
    <md>
        \xvec_{k} = \twovec{R_k}{S_k}\ \mbox{and}\ A = \left[\begin{array}{rr}a &amp; b \\ c &amp; d\end{array} \right],
    </md>
    then we see that we can express the equations for <m>R_{k+1}</m> and
    <m>S_{k+1}</m> in matrix-vector form by writing
    <md>
        \xvec_{k+1}= A\xvec_{k}
    </md>
</p>

<p>
    In the simplest scenario, we may have two species <m>R</m> and <m>S</m> that
    don't interact with one another.
    Some species can grow on their own without any support, while other species
    may die off.
    As one example, suppose that the populations of <m>R</m> and <m>S</m> are
    modeled by the dynamical system
    <md>
        \xvec_{k+1}= \left[\begin{array}{rr}1.2 &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; 0.9\end{array} \right]\xvec_{k}
    </md>
    and say the initial populations are given by
    <m>\xvec_{0} = \twovec{100}{200}</m>.
</p>

<p>
    This tells us that
    <md>
        \xvec_{1} = Ax_{0} = \left[\begin{array}{rr}1.2 &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; 0.9\end{array} \right] \twovec{100}{200}= \twovec{100 \cdot 1.2}{200 \cdot 0.9}
    </md>
    and thus
    <md>
        \xvec_{2} = Ax_{1} = \left[\begin{array}{rr}1.2 &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; 0.9\end{array} \right] \twovec{100 \cdot 1.2}{200 \cdot 0.9}= \twovec{100 \cdot 1.2^2}{200 \cdot 0.9^2}
    </md>
    It is quickly apparent that
    <md>
        \xvec_{k+1}= Ax_{k} = \left[\begin{array}{rr}1.2 &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; 0.9\end{array} \right] \twovec{100 \cdot 1.2^{k-1}}{200 \cdot 0.9^{k-1}}= \twovec{100 \cdot 1.2^k}{200 \cdot 0.9^k}
    </md>
</p>

<p>
    Observe further that if we focus on the components of <m>\xvec_{k+1}</m>, we
    see that
    <md>
        \xvec_{k+1}= \twovec{R_{k+1}}{S_{k+1}}= \twovec{100 \cdot 1.2^k}{200 \cdot 0.9^k}
    </md>
    from which we see that as <m>k \to \infty</m>, <m>R_{k+1}</m> grows without
    bound (since <m>1.2^{k}</m> grows exponentially), while <m>S_{k+1}\to 0</m>,
    since <m>0.9^{k}</m> decays exponentially to <m>0</m>.
</p>

<p>
    Note that the dynamical system above can also be viewed as saying that
    <m>\xvec_{k+1}= A\xvec_{k} = A(A\xvec_{k-1}) = A^{2} \xvec_{k-1}= \cdots = A^{k} \xvec_{0}</m>,
    so in some sense we are interested in powers of the matrix <m>A</m>.
    In this first example above, those powers are easy to understand because the
    matrix <m>A</m> is diagonal <ndash/> its only nonzero entries are on the
    diagonal.
</p>

<p>
    For a matrix <m>A</m> that is not diagonal (nor triangular), its powers are
    more complicated; we can instead use the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of the
    matrix to help us understand the long-term behavior of the dynamical system.
</p>

</introduction>

<page>

<exercise >
    <introduction>
            <p>
                Consider two species <m>R</m> and <m>S</m> that are modeled by
                the discrete dynamical system
                <md alignment="align">
                    <mrow>R_{k+1} &amp; = &amp; 0.9R_k + 0.8S_k</mrow>
                    <mrow>S_{k+1} &amp; = &amp; 0.2R_k + 0.9S_k</mrow>
                </md>
                so that the system is given by <m>\xvec_{k+1}= A\xvec_{k}</m>
                where
                <m>A = \left[\begin{array}{rr}0.9 &amp; 0.8 \\ 0.2 &amp; 0.9\end{array} \right]</m>.
            </p>
            </introduction>
                    <task workspace="2in">
                        <statement>
                        <p>
                            (2) Verify that <m>\vvec_{1} = \twovec21</m> and
                            <m>\vvec_{2} = \twovec{-2}{1}</m> are eigenvectors
                            of the matrix <m>A</m>, and find their corresponding
                            eigenvalues.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    </task>

                    <task workspace="1in">
                        <statement>
                        <p>
                            (2) Consider the initial population state
                            <m>\xvec_{0} = \twovec{100}{100}</m>, and write
                            <m>\xvec_{0}</m> as a linear combination of
                            <m>\vvec_{1} = \twovec21</m> and
                            <m>\vvec_{2} = \twovec{-2}{1}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    </task>

                    <task workspace="2in">
                        <p>
                            (2) Use the linearity of matrix-vector
                            multiplication (that is, the fact that
                            <m>A(c_{1} \vvec_{1} + c_{2} \vvec_{2}) = c_{1} A\vvec_{1} + c_{2} A\vvec_{2}</m>)
                            along with your results in (b) to write
                            <m>\xvec_{1} = A\xvec_{0}</m> as a linear
                            combination* of <m>\vvec_{1}</m> and
                            <m>\vvec_{2}</m>.
                            Do likewise for <m>\xvec_{2} = A\xvec_{1}</m>.
                            (* - in both of these, it's best <em>not</em> to
                            multiply the weights from (b) by the other constants
                            that arise, as this will help you see key patterns.)
                        </p>

                   
                    </task>

                    <task workspace="2in">
                        <p>
                            (2) Use your work in (c) to generalize the pattern
                            and write <m>\xvec_{k+1}</m> as a linear combination
                            of <m>\vvec_{1}</m> and <m>\vvec_{2}</m>.
                            State your formula for <m>\xvec_{k+1}</m> below.
                            What part of this expression will approach 0 as
                            <m>k \to \infty</m>?
                            Why?
                        </p>

                       
                    </task>

                    <task workspace="2in">
                        <p>
                            (3) Use your result in (d) to compute
                            <m>\xvec_{k} = \twovec{R_k}{S_k}</m> for
                            <m>k = 4, 5, 6</m> and state those vectors clearly
                            below, writing them both as linear combinations of
                            <m>\vvec_{1}</m> and <m>\vvec_{2}</m> <em>and</em>
                            as a single vector with numerical entries.
                            What do you observe about the ratio of <m>R_{k}</m>
                            to <m>S_{k}</m> as <m>k</m> increases?
                            How is this connected to the eigenvalues and
                            eigenvector of <m>A</m>?
                        </p>

                      
                    </task>

                    <task workspace="1in">
                        <p>
                            (2) Now revisit the original equations,
                            <md alignment="align">
                                <mrow>R_{k+1} &amp; = &amp; 0.9R_k + 0.8S_k</mrow>
                                <mrow>S_{k+1} &amp; = &amp; 0.2R_k + 0.9S_k</mrow>
                            </md>
                            Why do these equations tell us that these
                            populations have a symbiotic relationship (that is,
                            one where they help one another)?
                            How is this connected to the long-term behavior of
                            each?
                        </p>

                     
                    </task>
    
                </exercise>
            </page>
            <page>

        <exercise >
            <introduction>
               <p> Next, consider two different species <m>R</m> and <m>S</m> that
                are modeled by
                <md alignment="align">
                    <mrow>R_{k+1} &amp; = &amp; 0.7R_k + 0.5S_k</mrow>
                    <mrow>S_{k+1} &amp; = &amp; -0.4R_k + 1.6S_k</mrow>
                </md>
                so that the system is given by <m>\xvec_{k+1}= A\xvec_{k}</m>
                where
                <m>A = \left[\begin{array}{rr}0.7 &amp; 0.5 \\ -0.4 &amp; 1.6\end{array} \right]</m>.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        
                    <task workspace="1in">
                        <statement>
                        <p>
                            (2) Explain why we call this a <q>predator-prey</q>
                            system.
                            Which species, <m>R</m> or <m>S</m>, is the
                            predator?
                            Which is the prey?
                            Explain by discussing which species <q>helps</q> the
                            other, and which species <q>hurts</q> the other.
                        </p>

                    </statement>
                    </task>

                    <task workspace="1in">
                        <statement>
                        <p>
                            (2) Show that <m>\vvec_{1} = \twovec11</m> and
                            <m>\vvec_{2} = \twovec{5}{4}</m> are eigenvectors of
                            the matrix <m>A</m>, and find their corresponding
                            eigenvalues.
                        </p>

                    </statement>
                    </task>

                    <task workspace="1in">
                         <statement>
                        <p>
                            (1) Based on the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of
                            <m>A</m> and the work you did in question #1, do you
                            think that the populations <m>R</m> and <m>S</m> are
                            going to both grow, both die out, or have one grow
                            and the other die out?
                            Why?
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    </task>

                    <task workspace="1in">
                         <statement>
                        <p>
                            (2) Consider the initial population state
                            <m>\xvec_{0} = \twovec{150}{140}</m>, and write
                            <m>\xvec_{0}</m> as a linear combination of
                            <m>\vvec_{1} = \twovec11</m> and
                            <m>\vvec_{2} = \twovec{5}{4}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                     
                    </task>

                    <task workspace="1in">
                         <statement>
                        <p>
                            (2) Use the linearity of matrix-vector
                            multiplication along with your results in (b) and
                            (d) to write <m>\xvec_{1} = A\xvec_{0}</m> as a
                            linear combination* of <m>\vvec_{1}</m> and
                            <m>\vvec_{2}</m>.
                            Do likewise for <m>\xvec_{2} = A\xvec_{1}</m>.
                            (* - in both of these, it's best <em>not</em> to
                            multiply the weights from (d) by the other constants
                            that arise, as this will help you see key patterns.)
                        </p>

                    </statement>
                    </task>

                    <task workspace="1in">
                         <statement>
                        <p>
                            (3) Use your work in (e) to generalize the pattern
                            and write <m>\xvec_{k+1}</m> as a linear combination
                            of <m>\vvec_{1}</m> and <m>\vvec_{2}</m>.
                            State your formula for <m>\xvec_{k+1}</m> below.
                            What will be the long-term behavior of both
                            populations?
                            Why?
                        </p>

                    </statement>
                    </task>

                    <task workspace="1in">
                         <statement>
                        <p>
                            (5) Finally, suppose that instead of the
                            coefficients in the original predator-prey system of
                            this question, which were:
                            <md alignment="align">
                                <mrow>R_{k+1} &amp; = &amp; 0.7R_k + 0.5S_k</mrow>
                                <mrow>S_{k+1} &amp; = &amp; -0.4R_k + 1.6S_k</mrow>
                            </md>
                            we have
                            <md alignment="align">
                                <mrow>R_{k+1} &amp; = &amp; 0.5R_k + 0.2S_k</mrow>
                                <mrow>S_{k+1} &amp; = &amp; -0.4R_k + 1.1S_k</mrow>
                            </md>
                            For the matrix
                            <m>A = \left[\begin{array}{rr}0.5 &amp; 0.2 \\ -0.4 &amp; 1.1\end{array} \right]</m>,
                            it turns out that the eigenvectors are
                            <m>\vvec_{1} = \twovec12</m> and
                            <m>\vvec_{2} = \twovec11</m> with corresponding
                            eigenvalues <m>\lambda_{1} = 0.9</m> and
                            <m>\lambda_{2} = 0.7</m>.
                        </p>

                        <p>
                            For an initial population distribution of
                            <m>\xvec_{0} = \twovec{200}{300}</m>, write
                            <m>\xvec_{0}</m> as a linear combination of the
                            eigenvectors and find an expression for
                            <m>\xvec_{k+1}</m> in terms of the eigenvalues and
                            eigenvectors of <m>A</m>.
                            What will be the long-term behavior of the
                            populations for this particular scenario?
                            Why?
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    </task>
                </exercise>
            </page>
